          %h1#talks_in_more_detail Talks in more detail
          %h4#schick
            Thomas Schick,
            %em Smooth cohomology theories
          %p
            The basic idea of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            (also called
            %em smooth cohomology
            ) is to combine generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            (e.g. ordinary integral cohomology, or K-theory, or tmf, or …) and differential forms. Hopkins and Singer showed that a smooth refinement exists for any generalized cohomology theory, but didn’t provide an (easy for mere mortals to understand) explicit construction. In this talk, Thomas Schick advertised geometric models for multiplicative smooth cohomology with
            = succeed "-integration" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %h5#resources Resources
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_schick.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Schick)"} Notes by Bruce Bartlett
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.sunysb.edu/~blafard/tex/sum09/ow_june_9.pdf"} Notes by Gabriel Drummond-Cole
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Tuesday%2C+June+9"} Notes by Urs Schreiber
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_pavlov_schick.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Schick) by Pavlov"} Notes by Dmitry Pavlov
          %h4#bunke
            Ulrich Bunke,
            = succeed "." do
              %em Smooth K-theory
          %p
            In this talk, which led on from the previous one by Thomas Schick, Ulrich Bunke gave an explicit description of their smooth
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{E}, \rho)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            is a ‘geometric family’, namely a smooth proper submersion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \rightarrow B
            with a fiberwise metric, a Clifford bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            and some connection of some sort. The object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            is an element of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(B, K) = C^\infty(B, \Lambda^*T^* B \otimes K^*)
            = succeed "-orientatiosn" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "-invariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
          %h5#resources_2 Resources
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_bunke.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Bunke)"} Notes by Bruce Bartlett
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.sunysb.edu/~blafard/tex/sum09/ow_june_9.pdf"} Notes by Gabriel Drummond-Cole
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Tuesday%2C+June+9"} Notes by Urs Schreiber
          %h4#schweigert Christoph Schweigert and Ingo Runkel,_CFT and algebra in braided tensor categories I and II_.
          %p
            This talk explains how correlators for a rational two-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            can be constructed in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} functorial TFT
            formalism. Based on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and the correlator, as an element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi tft
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tft_C (\hat{X})
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{X}
            a double cover of the surface
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_X
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial M_X = \hat{X}
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+class+group"} mapping class group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sewing+constraint"} sewing constraint
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalent
            special symemtric Frobenius algebras lead to an equivalent description of the correlators. A Morita invariant formulation is provided by the notion of a module category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            over the module tensor category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} world sheet
            is now decorated by categories, functors between module categories and natural transformations.
          %h5#resources_3 Resources
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Notes by Bruce Bartlett
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_schweigert.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Schweigert)"} 1
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_runkel.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Runkel)"} 2
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.sunysb.edu/~blafard/tex/sum09/ow_june_8.pdf"} Notes by Gabriel Drummond-Cole
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Monday%2C+June+8"} Notes by Urs Schreiber
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_pavlov_schweigert_runkel.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Schweigert Runkel) by Pavlov"} Notes by Dmitry Pavlov
          %h4#costello
            Kevin Costello,
            = succeed "." do
              %em Factorization algebras in perturbative QFT
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"} factorization algebra
            on a manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is an object which associates to every ball
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subseteq M
            a vector space (or cochain complex)
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1 \coprod \cdots \coprod B_n \subset B_{n+1}
            of disjoint balls in a larger ball, a map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(B_1) \otimes \cdots \otimes F(B_n) \rightarrow F(B_{n+1})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
            analogues of chiral algebras, as introduced by Beilinson and Drinfeld; they are closely related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+operad"} algebras
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/little+n-disk+operad"} little n-disk operad
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_n
          %p
            In this series of lectures, Kevin Costello set up a framework to construct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"} factorization algebras
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            picture of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            are encoded in an associative algebra, Costello argues that the observables of a QFT on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            are encoded in a factorization algebra on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
          %p This series of lectures finished with the statement of a theorem allowing one to quantize the commutative factorization algebra associated to a classical field theory in a range of situations, including situations of physical interest. This is joint work with Owen Gwilliam.
          %h5#resources_4 Resources
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Notes by Bruce Bartlett
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_costello1.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Costello 1)"} 1
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_costello2.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Costello 2)"} 2
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_costello2.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Costello 2)"} 3
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.sunysb.edu/~blafard/tex/sum09/ow_june_8.pdf"} Notes by Gabriel Drummond-Cole 1
                and
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.sunysb.edu/~blafard/tex/sum09/ow_june_9.pdf"} 2
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Monday%2C+June+8"} Notes by Urs Schreiber 1
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Tuesday%2C+June+9"} 2
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_pavlov_costello.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Costello) by Pavlov"} Notes by Dmitry Pavlov
          %h4#drummond-cole
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel+C.+Drummond-Cole"}> Gabriel C. Drummond-Cole
            ,
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                \-Operads,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi BU
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BU_\infty
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Hypercomm
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hypercomm_\infty
          %p
            Gabriel Drummond-Cole applies the machinery of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} overbar
                  %mo ∂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial-\overbar{\partial}
            lemma to hypercommutative algebras (Frobenius manifolds). This is joint work with Bruno Valette. The following theorem was proved:
          .un_thm
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              be a differential BV-algebra over a field of characteristic zero. Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              be its homology. Then: 1. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              satisfies the noncommutative Hodge to de Rham degeneration condition, then there exists a hypercommutative
              = succeed "-structure" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              satisfies Park’s semiclassical condition then this structure is unique up to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hypercomm
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hypercomm_\infty
              quasi-isomorphism.
          %h5#resources_5 Resources
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord
                  oberwolfach_june2009_pavlov.pdf
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_pavlov.pdf"} ?
            %li
              %p
                Gabriel Drummond-Cole’s
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.sunysb.edu/~blafard/"} website
          %h4#kahle
            Alex Kahle,
            = succeed "." do
              %em Superconnections and index theory
          %p
            In this talk, Alex Kahle described results from his thesis work (his supervisor was Dan Freed) about
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0810.0820"} superconnections and index theory
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconnection"} superconnection
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} ordinary connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \rightarrow 0
            to a certain differential form) needs to be modified though, because forms of different degrees have different scaling behaviour (recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconnection"} superconnection
            involves forms of different degrees). Alex worked out how to scale everything correctly so that one indeed gets a local index theorem for superconnections, leading to many potential applications.
          %h5#resources_6 Resources
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_kahle.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Kahle)"} Notes by Bruce Bartlett
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.sunysb.edu/~blafard/tex/sum09/ow_june_10.pdf"} Notes by Gabriel Drummond-Cole
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Wednesday%2C+June+10"} Notes by Urs Schreiber
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_pavlov_kahle.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Kahle) by Pavlov"} Notes by Dmitry Pavlov
          %h4#schommer-pries
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Schommer-Pries"}> Chris Schommer-Pries
            ,
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                Topological defects,
                = succeed "-branes," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            Chris explained his classification result mentioned at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (infinity,n)-category of cobordisms
            from his thesis of extended (he suggested calling them
            = succeed ")" do
              %em local
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Cob
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Cob
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher-categorical viewpoint
            unites the following two ideas: the ‘open-closed’ theories and the ‘field theories with defects’ from Ingo Runkel and Christoph Schweigert’s talk. He showed how both these concepts are particular examples of a single notion, namely that of a collection of natural transformations between a restriction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            = succeed "-functors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            This is precisely the kind of viewpoint that Urs Schreiber and Jens Fjelstad
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/08/dbranes_from_tin_cans_part_x.html"} suggested
            at the
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/"} n-category cafe
            under the slogal
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/10/dbranes_from_tin_cans_arrow_th.html"} D-branes from tin-cans
            (a D-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
            is a boundary condition ordefect, a “tin-can diagram” is the naturality diagram for a higher natural transformation).
          %blockquote
            %p
              Cf. at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle+of+higher+category+theory"} holographic principle of higher category theory
              and at
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+functorial+field+theory"} twisted functorial field theory
          %h5#resources_7 Resources
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://sites.google.com/site/chrisschommerpriesmath/Home/Slides-MFO-6-11-09.pdf?attredirects=0"} Slides on webpage
          %h4#freed
            Dan Freed,
            = succeed "." do
              %em Geometry and topology of orientifolds
          %p
            Freed described recent work with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Distler"} Jacques Distler
            and Greg Moore on a certain background structure in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} superstring theory
            called an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifold"} orientifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe
            on a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            with a peculiar “twisted”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance"} equivariance
            condition. There is apparantly a beautiful string theory formula to calculate the ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            = succeed "\u2019," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge"} charge
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi RR
                  %mi charge
                  %mo =
                  %mo ±
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi something
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi L
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi L
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ν
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  RR charge = \pm 2^{something} i_* \sqrt\left( \frac{L'(F)}{L'(\nu)} \right)
          %p
            involving a certain normal bundle and the ‘Bott element in K-theory’, which looks similar to the Hirzebruch formula for the L-genus. This uses some kind of twisted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            version of KR theory. The whole thing can be viewed as a new sort of anomaly, having to do with an exotic orientation of some kind.
