                  oberwolfach_june2009_pavlov_henriques.pdf
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_pavlov_henriques.pdf"} ?
          %h4#andr_henriques_evening_informal_session_on_more_details_on_conformal_nets André Henriques, Evening informal session on more details on conformal nets.
          %p André responded to questions from the audience about further details regarding the ‘conformal nets’ programme.
          %h5#resources_13 Resources
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_henriques_extra.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Henriques extra)"} Notes by Bruce Bartlett
            %li
              %p
                Write-up about conformal nets on André Henriques’
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.uu.nl/people/henrique/"} webpage
          %h4#waldorf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konrad+Waldorf"}> Konrad Waldorf
            ,
            = succeed "." do
              %em String connections and Chern-Simons 2-gerbes
          %p
            Konrad described the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+2-gerbe"} Chern-Simons 2-gerbe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi CS
                    %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CS_P
            which is a geometric object living on any manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            which is equipped with a Spin-bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            of the basic bundle gerbe over the Spin group. He demonstrates that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"} string structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a
            %em trivialization
            of this 2-gerbe, in the sense of a morphism from the trivial 2-gerbe living over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi CS
                      %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CS_P
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{2} p_1(P) = 0
            %em string connection
            on a string structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (a connection on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is needed here) on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            as a connection on this trivialization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            which is compatible with various things.
          %p
            Basically, the data of a string structure together with a string connection on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a class in the
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi CS
                      %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [CS_P]
              \-twisted
            differential cohomology of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            in Urs’s sense, for vanishing twist (the twist is the nontriviality of the String structure). Konrad described various results about string connections, such as the fact that they form a contractible space, confirming a conjecture by Stolz and Teichner. See his recent
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0906.0117"} arXiv article
          %h5#resources_14 Resources
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_waldorf.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Waldorf)"} Notes by Bruce Bartlett
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.sunysb.edu/~blafard/tex/sum09/ow_june_11.pdf"} Notes by Gabriel Drummond-Cole
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Thursday%2C+June+11"} Notes by Urs Schreiber
          %h4#walker
            Kevin Walker,
            = succeed "." do
              %em Blob homology
          %p
            Kevin Walker described a new way to think about
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} extended TQFTs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            he defined a ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/blob+complex"}> blob complex
            ’
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_*(M, C)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(M)
            to manifolds of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq n
            are produced out of this chain complex, with the Atiyah-Segal axioms following as corollaries of the setup. He listed a number of examples of the TQFT’s he has in mind in the beginning, such as the finite group model, one based on a
            %em
              \-algebra, one based on a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pivotal+category"} pivotal category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ribbon+category"} ribbon category
          %h5#resources_15 Resources
          %ul
            %li
              Slides available on
              %a{:href => "http://canyon23.net/math"} webpage
          %h4#cohen
            Ralph Cohen,
            %em String topology, field theories and Fukaya categories.
          %p
            This talk was about relating the subject of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+topology"} string topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} String theory
            was described as a ‘homological
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+theory"} Gromov-Witten symplectic field theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*M
            is equivalent to string topology of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %h5#resources_16 Resources
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/oberwolfach_june2009_waldorf.pdf", :title => "Oberwolfach June 2009 (Waldorf)"} Notes by Bruce Bartlett
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.sunysb.edu/~blafard/tex/sum09/ow_june_12.pdf"} Notes by Gabriel Drummond-Cole
          %h4#redden
            Corbett Redden,
            = succeed "." do
              %em String structures, 3-forms, and tmf classes
          %p
            Corbett began my defining a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"} string structure
            on a manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            as a certain lift of a classifying map; string structures up to homotopy are canonically isomorphic with things called ‘string classes’ on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            \- namely, elements in the third integral cohomology of the total space of the bundle which behave on the fibers as the generator of the third cohomology of the Spin group. The point about string structures is that they transgress to give a spin structure on the loop space of the manifold, which is what one wants to study things like the Witten genus. He showed a beautiful way to obtain string structures from a metric on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mi n
